{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfhyowRYxSk6PbAWAtGxQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote the candidate `gamblers_chance_vs_motif` and it passes the self-check (`OK`). On the short 100-draw, 1-chain smoke fit its ELPD-LOO is −989.5; the incumbent's full fit is −995.1. That comparison is only a hint, because a smoke fit that small isn't a real score.\n\n**What I refined:** the incumbent `length_normalised_chance_vs_motif`. I changed one thing: people's model of chance itself. Before, the fair coin switched sides with one fixed, over-alternating probability. Now it is a gambler's-fallacy coin whose chance of switching rises with the length of the current run, with one new fitted slope. Everything else is the incumbent's, unchanged: the three regular generators, the per-flip length normalisation and each person's own sensitivity.\n\n**Why this change:** it targets two discrepancies in `critiques.md` that both survive the FDR correction:\n- **Long streaks are under-penalised.** Among pairs with about the same number of switches, people avoid the sequence with the longer run more than the incumbent predicts. Under the new chance model a long run is unlikely beyond what its switch count implies, so the model can now tell such pairs apart.\n- **Perfect alternation is over-credited.** People pick it less often than the incumbent predicts (observed 0.768 vs predicted 0.827). Now that the slope carries the push towards switching, a sequence where every run is one flip long only earns the baseline switch rate.\n\n**Fix during the check:** the first run failed with a non-finite ELPD-LOO. The log-probabilities of switching underflowed to −∞ at long run lengths, and multiplying that by a zero count gave NaN. I now compute them in a numerically stable way, narrowed the slope's prior to Normal(0, 0.5), and clipped `p_left` to stay strictly between 0 and 1.\n\n**Still unchecked:**\n- **Pareto k:** on the smoke fit 11% of trials have a high Pareto k, so PSIS-LOO counted as unreliable there. Small smoke fits often do this; whether the full fit is reliable is unknown.\n- **Novelty, convergence, speed:** the check doesn't test whether the model is different enough from the live ones, or whether it converges and finishes within admission's 30-minute limit. The smoke fit took 67 seconds.\n\nFiles are in `/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run3/data/subjective_randomness/experiment1/model_loop/iter_4/candidate_3/`:\n- `hypothesis.md`\n- `model_name.txt`\n- `candidate.py`"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1639,"cache_read_input_tokens":76421,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1639,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bf4f6d6a-e6df-46b7-811d-ef0cd0f255bc","uuid":"bf95d502-01f4-4379-9e2e-8633fef24c2c","timestamp":"2026-10-04T22:24:19.416Z","request_id":"req_011CfhyovUzsTnBTEn1YUEKQ"}
